 ## Overview
-Isosurface Color Enhancement is a shading technique applied to intersection points on an isosurface to illustrate neighborhood density and reveal internal structures behind the isosurface [S1, unofficial]. It assigns material color by approximating the accumulated alpha in a semi-transparent neighborhood behind the surface, using the scalar changing rate (directional derivative) as a proxy for neighborhood thickness [S1, unofficial]. Unlike full volume rendering, which requires intensive calculation and many samples, this method maintains the simplicity and performance efficiency of standard isosurface rendering without increasing computational complexity [S1, unofficial].
+Isosurface Color Enhancement is a shading technique applied to intersection points on an isosurface to illustrate neighborhood density and reveal internal structures behind the isosurface [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard isosurface rendering which assigns a constant or gradient-based color, this method approximates the accumulated alpha in a semi-transparent neighborhood behind the surface using the scalar changing rate as a proxy for neighborhood thickness [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart by maintaining the simplicity and computational complexity of standard isosurface rendering without increasing the voxel-traversal or ray-voxel intersection cost [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs**
-*   **Input**: Scalar field $V$, isovalue $l_{iso}$, viewing ray direction $\vec{r}$, local transfer function defined by pairs $\{\alpha_{T,i}, \tilde{c}_i\}_{i=1}^n$ where $\alpha_{T,i}$ is opacity and $\tilde{c}_i=(r_i, g_i, b_i)$ is color, global parameters `densityFactor`, `stdSampleDistance`, and optional $\Delta v$ for deep neighborhood estimation.
-*   **Output**: Enhanced color $\tilde{C}$ for the intersection point $\mathbf{p}$.
+*   **Input:** Scalar field $v$, isovalue $l_{iso}$, viewing ray direction $\vec{r}$, local transfer function defined by pairs $\{\alpha_{T,i}, \tilde{c}_i\}_{i=1}^n$ where $\alpha_{T,i}$ is opacity and $\tilde{c}_i=(r_i, g_i, b_i)$ is color, global parameters `densityFactor` and $\Delta v$, and sample distance `stdSampleDistance`.
+*   **Output:** Final color $\tilde{C}$ for the intersection point $\mathbf{p}$.
 
 **Data Structures**
-*   **Speed-color Map**: A logarithmically sampled array of size $m$. The $j$-th entry stores the pre-calculated color $\tilde{C}_j$ for speed value $\text{speed}_j = -\ln(1 - j/m)$, where $j = 1, 2, \dots, m$ [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Speed-color Map:** A logarithmically sampled array of size $m$. The $j$-th entry stores the pre-calculated color $\tilde{C}_j$ for speed value $\text{speed}_j = -\ln(1 - j/m)$, where $j = 1, 2, ..., m$ [S1, unofficial].
 
